{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And her neighbor.</b> From the one who dwelt with her in the house." "<b>And you shall strip.</b> As the Targum renders: ״וּתְרוֹקִנוּן״ — and so ״וַיְנַצְּלוּ אֶת מִצְרַיִם״ (Exodus 12), ״וַיִּתְנַצְּלוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת עֶדְיָם״ (ibid. 33); and the nun in it is a root letter. Menachem associated it with the conjunction of צָדִי in ״וַיַּצֵּל אֱלֹהִים אֶת מִקְנֵה אֲבִיכֶם״, ״אֲשֶׁר הִצִּיל אֱלֹהִים מֵאָבִינוּ״ (Genesis 31) — but his words are not trustworthy, for if the nun were not a root letter and it were pointed with chirik, it would not function in the active-voice pattern but in the reflexive-passive pattern, like ״וְנִסַּחְתֶּם מֵעַל הָאֲדָמָה״ (Deuteronomy 28), ״וְנִתַּתֶּם בְּיַד אוֹיֵב״, ״וְנִגַּפְתֶּם לִפְנֵי אוֹיְבֵיכֶם״ (Leviticus 26), ״וְנִתַּכְתֶּם בְּתוֹכָהּ״ (Ezekiel 22), ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם נִצַּלְנוּ״ (Jeremiah 7) — reflexive-passive voice. And every nun that comes at the beginning of a word and is separable from it — like the nun of נוֹגֵף, נוֹשֵׂא, נוֹתֵן, נוֹשֵׂךְ — when it forms the active-voice pattern, is pointed with a shewa, like ״וּנְשָׂאתֶם אֶת אֲבִיכֶם״ (Genesis 45), ״וּנְתַתֶּם לָהֶם אֶת אֶרֶץ הַגִּלְעָד״ (Numbers 32), ״וּנְמַלְתֶּם אֵת בְּשַׂר עָרְלַתְכֶם״ (Genesis 17). Therefore I say that this chirik belongs to the root — and the root is the noun נִצּוּל, and it belongs to the 'heavy' patterns, like דִּבּוּר, כִּפּוּר, לִמּוּד; when conjugated in the active-voice pattern, it is pointed with chirik, like ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֶל הַסֶּלַע״ (Numbers 20), ״וְכִפַּרְתֶּם אֶת הַבַּיִת״ (Ezekiel 45), ״וְלִמַּדְתֶּם אוֹתָם אֶת בְּנֵיכֶם״ (Deuteronomy 11)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:50:35.538449727Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}